  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =10$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10.64<X<18.28) & =P(\\frac{10.64-10}{4}<Z<\\frac{18.28-10}{4}) \\\\  & =P(0.16<Z<2.07).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/dc1c1e8b01372821c4ad13bfb1bbfd2a102bb9d247815ace25219d3a3f42c8c2.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\u03a6(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =P(Z<2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =\u03a6(2.07)-\u03a6(0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03a6(2.07) & =0.9808 \\\\ \u03a6(0.16) & =0.5636\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =\u03a6(2.07)-\u03a6(0.16) \\\\  & =0.9808-0.5636 \\\\  & =0.4172.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(10.64<X<18.28)=0.4172.\n$$",
